| S. Akella, N. Iizuka, A. Miyata, *Genuine Multi-Entropy in the Toric Code*, arXiv:2607.06050v1, Appendix A, Eqs. (52)–(56) | `literature-attested`: the replica definition of $Z_n^{(\mathtt q)}$ and $S_n^{(\mathtt q)}$ with normalisation $\frac{1}{1-n}\frac{1}{n^{\mathtt q-2}}$ and the last party unshifted (Convention 2.2). |
| S. Akella, A. Gadde, J. Pandey, *Multi-invariants in stabilizer states*, arXiv:2601.16258v1, §3.1 and §5 | `literature-attested`: the Rényi entropies of a stabilizer state across a bipartition do not depend on the Rényi index (§3.1; Remark 3.3); for $\mathtt q=3$ and every replica index, the Rényi multi-entropy of a stabilizer state is given in §5 in terms of orders of stabilizer subgroups, via the GHZ-extraction theorem. The GHZ-extraction method for tripartite invariants and the resulting $n\to1$ multi-entropy formula are due to S. Akella, *Tripartite entanglement in the HaPPY code is not holographic*, arXiv:2510.08520v1, §3.2, Eq. (31), which treats only the limit $n\to1$; the formula for every replica index is the one in §5 of arXiv:2601.16258. This is the literature status of the values in the case $\mathtt q=3$ of Theorem 3.1 and Corollary 4.2; arXiv:2601.16258 states no rank formula over $\mathbb F_2(\zeta)$ and treats no $\mathtt q\ge4$ at $n\ge3$. Its multi-entropy normalisation omits the factor $n^{2-\mathtt q}$, which is immaterial for $\mathtt q\le2$ and a constant factor for $\mathtt q=3$. |
| N. Iizuka, S. Lin, *Symmetry-resolved genuine multi-entropy: Haar random and graph states*, arXiv:2511.00905v2, §III.2.1, Eqs. (61)–(69), and Appendix B | `literature-attested`: for a graph state with three parties $A,B,C$, Eq. (64), derived in Appendix B from the stabilizer-subgroup count of Bravyi et al. (Eq. (62)), gives the number of extractable GHZ states as $p=\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb F_2}\Gamma_{A:BC}+\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb F_2}\Gamma_{B:AC}+\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb F_2}\Gamma_{C:AB}-\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb F_2}\tilde\Gamma$, where $\tilde\Gamma$ is $\Gamma$ with its diagonal blocks removed; in the notation of Convention 2.5 this is $p=\tfrac12\big(r(A|BC)+r(B|AC)+r(C|AB)\big)-r(A|B|C)$. Combined with GHZ extraction and the GHZ and Bell-pair values of $S_n^{(3)}$, this gives the masked-rank form of the case $\mathtt q=3$ of Corollary 4.2; the source itself states the resulting entropy only for the genuine multi-entropy at $n=3$ (Eq. (66)). For four parties it conjectures, from numerics at $n=2$, a rank expression for the genuine multi-entropy (Eqs. (67)–(69)); no case $n\ge3$ with $\mathtt q\ge4$ is treated. |
| B. Czech, Y. Feng, X. Wu, M. Xie, *Fun with Graph States: Nonlocal Bell pairs and the Arf Invariant*, arXiv:2606.06582v2, §2.1 Eq. (11), §4.1 Eq. (40), §5.1, Appendix B (Eqs. (130)–(131)) and §B.2 | Closest prior work. `literature-attested`: for any graph $G$ on $M$ vertices with adjacency matrix $A$, $\langle+|^{\otimes M}|G\rangle$ is zero or equals $(-1)^\Delta2^{-\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb F_2}A/2}$ with $\Delta$ the Arf invariant of the quadratic refinement $x^{\mathsf T}A^{\mathrm{upper}}x$ (Eqs. (11), (40)); since $Z_n^{(\mathtt q)}$ (Eqs. (130)–(131), same normalisation as Convention 2.2) is an inner product of two graph states on $Nn^{\mathtt q-1}$ qubits, this gives a general $\mathbb F_2$-rank-and-Arf method for $Z_n^{(\mathtt q)}$ of graph states at every $n$ and $\mathtt q$, and §5.1 gives a second, cycle-based formula for expectation values of permutations. §B.1 computes the multi-invariants of the three-qubit chain; §B.2 computes $S_n^{(4)}$ of the four-qubit path graph with one qubit per party at $n=2,3,4$ (ranks $18,76,198$, $\Delta=0$; $S_3^{(4)}=\tfrac{19}{9}\log2$; the rank $76$ at $n=3$ agrees with $\sum_{t\ne0}\rho_t=76$ from Theorem 3.1), and states $S_n^{(4)}=(2+n^{-2})\log2$ for all $n$ only conditionally on an unpublished polynomiality result. The source does not contain: the reduction of that rank to the ranks $\rho_t$ of $N\times N$ matrices over $\mathbb F_2(\zeta)$ and the positivity of $Z_n^{(\mathtt q)}$ for all odd $n$ and all $\mathtt q$ (Theorem 3.1); the masked-rank identity for characters with at most three values and the partition decomposition for $\mathtt q\ge4$ (Proposition 4.1, Corollary 4.2); or the failure of the masked-rank expression (Proposition 5.1). |
| M. Hein, J. Eisert, H. J. Briegel, *Multiparty entanglement in graph states*, Phys. Rev. A 69 (2004) 062311, arXiv:quant-ph/0307130, Proposition 3, Eq. (43) | `literature-attested`: the base-$2$ logarithm of the Schmidt rank of a graph state across a bipartition equals $\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb F_2}\Gamma_{XY}$; with the preceding row this is the literature status of the case $\mathtt q=2$ (Remark 3.3). |
| M. Van den Nest, J. Dehaene, B. De Moor, *Graphical description of the action of local Clifford transformations on graph states*, Phys. Rev. A 69 (2004) 022316, arXiv:quant-ph/0308151, Theorem 1 | `literature-attested`: every stabilizer state is equivalent to a graph state under local Clifford operations (Convention 2.3). |
